WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.000
Oh, hey crazies. I didn't see you there.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.320
As you can see, I’m baking some cookies.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.180
You know, this reminds me of something.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:18.440
Where was I?

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:19.840
Oh right! Cookies!

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:24.360
If we tried to guess how they were invented,
we’d imagine some genius of food realizing

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.939
exactly what to do.
or, at the very least, someone with a lot

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:30.840
of time and money just randomly mixing ingredients

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.480
and setting oven temperatures until something works.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:39.080
When, in reality, this beautiful creation
came to be through an insanely slow process.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:41.720
First, there was just bread and crackers.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:45.960
Then, in the 7th century CE, Persia discovered
sugar cane,

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.460
which quickly spread across the Middle East
and eventually Europe.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:53.320
Then came cakes from the Old Norse word kaka.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:57.420
Then came something kind of like a cookie
or Koekje in Dutch,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:01.420
meaning “little cakes,”
which were probably originally used to test

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:04.440
oven temperature so bakers didn’t waste
a whole cake.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:10.160
It wasn’t until 1930 that some random person
in Massachusetts ran out of nuts and, on a

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:13.159
whim, mixed in crumbled bits of chocolate
instead.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:17.240
The point is: No single person can be credited
for the invention of the cookie.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:21.240
It took a series of accidents and thousands
of people spanning many centuries

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:22.700
and several continents.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:24.960
Discovery is almost never sudden.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.000
It takes time.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.680
But humans have trouble with time.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.380
I’m not talking about time dilation
at high speeds or near black holes.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.580
Those are actual physical changes to the passage
of time

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:38.520
and most of us could never hope to experience
them in any noticeable way.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.440
No, what I’m talking about is our perception
of time,

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.920
which is deeply dependent on our biology and
psychology.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:48.460
You know;
brain processing, sensory signals, amounts

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.200
of sleep, emotional states
drug use, even age!

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:53.720
Let’s start with the smaller issues.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:58.540
We've talked about whether Planck time is a single moment in time, but that's still a matter of debate,

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:04.120
For a human, a psychological moment is
about 1/10 of second or 100 milliseconds,

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:05.120
on average.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.999
It can vary a little throughout the day or
from day to day,

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.940
but there’s an easy way to test it with
a ruler.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:13.040
Have someone hold a ruler in your hand, like
this.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.680
Without warning you, or psyching you out,
they release the ruler.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.740
The ruler will fall before you have chance
to catch it.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:23.080
However far that is will tell you how long
it was falling.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.540
That’s what we call your "reaction time."

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:30.800
For an average human on an average day, that’s
about 1/5 of a second or 200 milliseconds.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.820
I thought you said it was 100 milliseconds.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.680
That’s because your reaction time is 2 moments.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:38.100
One for your brain to realize something is
happening

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.640
and another for your brain to do something
about it,

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.540
so half your reaction time is one psychological
moment for you.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:45.500
ish.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:50.900
In that 100 milliseconds, your brain can play around
with timing a little so that causality makes sense.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:54.620
Like lining up what you hear and feel with
what you see.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:57.180
After that, you have a limited attention span.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:02.320
Transient attention is your subconscious attention
on something that suddenly changed somewhere

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:03.500
in your field of view.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.200
It only lasts a few seconds.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:10.240
Selective sustained attention is when you
consciously chose to focus on a task.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.040
It can last anywhere from 5 to 20 minutes.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.760
After that, you have to choose to refocus
on the task.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:21.700
The point is: we’re hard-wired to deal well
with seconds, minutes, hours, and days.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:24.700
For months and years, we need to rely on our
memory

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:26.390
and that can be a little tricky.

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:30.100
If we learn something, but it doesn’t continue
to be useful or to seem important,

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:31.730
your brain’s not going to hold onto it.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:34.140
It’s part of why classes have assignments.

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:38.580
It’s why advertising gets shoved in your
face over and over and over.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:42.000
Politicians use it hoping you’ll forget
their indiscretions

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.300
and their opponents do everything they can
to remind you of them.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:48.980
Even when we do remember events, we don’t
always accurately remember

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:50.800
how long ago they occurred.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.540
Our memory is pretty useful if you’re a
hunter-gatherer trying to avoid dangers like tigers

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:57.100
or poisonous plants.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:00.700
But it makes us essentially blind to things
that can take decades or more,

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:01.900
like global warming.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:06.120
Anything that seems to last longer than a
human lifespan, we call “permanent.”

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.160
Like magnets, or trees, or even cities.

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:13.640
When it comes to the really long-term stuff
like history, we have to tell stories.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:16.260
We like there to be a narrative, for there
to be purpose.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:20.360
So, when someone tells you a cookie came into
existence through a series of accidents,

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:23.040
it’s perfectly natural to stand there in disbelief.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.980
It’s called "confirmation bias."

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:29.740
You reject the new information because it
doesn’t fit into your preexisting experience.

00:04:29.749 --> 00:04:31.990
A single accident doesn’t appear to do much.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:33.180
We can’t see its effects.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:35.640
But a bunch of accidents happening over centuries?

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.040
Well, that brought us the cookie.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:39.940
If a few centuries of accidents can make a
cookie, then

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:44.719
it shouldn’t be surprising to find 100 million
years of evolutionary accidents

00:04:44.719 --> 00:04:46.860
turned a small mammal into a human.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:52.169
It shouldn’t be that surprising, somewhere
in a vast universe, billions of years of accidents made

00:04:52.169 --> 00:04:55.020
a solar system, where we happen to be able
to live.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:59.040
These time scales are completely incomprehensible
for the human brain.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:02.920
That’s why we have to rely on physical evidence
and the scientific process.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:05.980
Without them, we can’t possibly hope to
see the whole picture.

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:08.100
Hmm, I need another cookie.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:15.960
So, the next time you have a cookie,
maybe think about evolution for a moment.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.660
Mmm, delicious, delicious evolution.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:21.139
Let me know in the comments what your favorite type of cookie is.

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:24.300
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

