00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:52.810
the first place.

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:54.949
Sure, take the fun out of it.

00:05:54.949 --> 00:05:59.180
David suggests science isn't about “right”
or “wrong,” but about creating models.

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:01.560
This is important, so we’re going to spend
some time on it.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:06.400
Whether we like it or not, the universe is
mostly spectrums. It's mostly grey area.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:11.260
Anything you think is black or white has probably
been imposed on the world by people.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:16.280
It is also true that a goal of science is
to create models so we can make accurate predictions

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:17.340
about reality.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:20.940
Which model you choose depends on how much
error you’re comfortable with.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:24.150
The Earth is pretty flat if you’re only
looking at a mile or two.

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:28.460
Newton’s laws work great until you go really
fast or near a black hole.

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:34.380
No model predicts anything perfectly,
so I would agree there is no absolute accuracy,

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:36.159
but that doesn’t mean you can’t be wrong.

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:39.540
If science were only about models and predictions,
it would be pretty boring.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:44.600
Luckily, another goal of science is to see the big
picture, to get a deeper understanding.

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:47.420
Correct and accurate are two very different
things.

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:50.720
In the grand scheme, there are some absolutes.

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:52.120
The Earth is not flat.

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:53.120
It is a ball.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:53.800
Period.

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:57.760
It might not be exactly spherical, but it’s
definitely a ball.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:00.900
Those absolutes might be vague, but they’re
still absolutes

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:04.720
And, the more science we do, the less vague
those absolutes become.

